---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../../config.mjs';
import { cameras as allCameras, countsBy, registryDateOf, sources } from '../../lib/data.mjs';
import { curatedRoads } from '../../lib/pages.mjs';
import { roadSlug } from '../../../../ops/importers/lib/registry.mjs';

const cameras = allCameras.filter((c) => c.regionSlug === 'california'); // road pages are California-only
const registryDate = registryDateOf('california');
const roads = curatedRoads(cameras);
const byRoad = countsBy(cameras, 'road');
const source = sources.find((s) => s.id === 'caltrans');
---
<Base title={`Traffic cameras by road: ${roads.length} California, United States roads | ${SITE_NAME}`} description={`The ${roads.length} California roads with the most traffic cameras, each with its cameras in driving order and the closures, signs and chain controls between them.`} route="/roads/" crumbs={[{ name: 'Roads', route: '/roads/' }]}>
  <h1>Traffic cameras by road</h1>
  <p>Road pages exist for California, United States only (Caltrans data; British Columbia roads are not I-, SR- or US- roads). Each road below has its own page: every Caltrans camera on that road in driving order for each direction, then the lane closures, chain controls and message signs on the road, each with the cameras closest to it. The roads are the {roads.length} with the most cameras in our list ({cameras.length} cameras in all), each with at least 3. Not sure how to read one? See <a href="/guides/how-to-read-a-road-page/">how to read a road page</a>.</p>
  <section data-block="roads" data-basis="registry" data-source={source.name} data-asof={registryDate}>
    <h2>Roads with the most cameras</h2>
    <ul class="cols">{roads.map((r) => <li><a href={`/roads/${roadSlug(r)}/`}>{r} traffic cameras</a> ({byRoad[r]})</li>)}</ul>
  </section>
  <p><a class="cta" href="/map/">Open the map</a> <a href="/cameras/">Browse by city instead</a></p>
</Base>
